Release archives (hyperbutler-<version>-windows-amd64.zip) also contain a Linux guest image
(vmlinuz-virt, initramfs-stage1.gz and system.vhd), built by
.github/workflows/guest-image.yml from the signed Alpine Linux packages pinned in
guest/prototype/sources.lock, and nothing else besides hyperbutler's own guest binaries.
It is a separate program that hyperbutler.exe runs in a VM, distributed alongside
hyperbutler (mere aggregation); its components keep their own licenses, among them:

- Linux kernel and modules (Alpine linux-virt): GPL-2.0-only
- BusyBox (busybox-static): GPL-2.0-only
- e2fsprogs, e2fsprogs-libs and libcom_err: GPL-2.0-or-later AND LGPL-2.0-or-later AND
  BSD-3-Clause AND MIT
- libblkid: LGPL-2.1-or-later; libuuid: BSD-3-Clause (util-linux); libeconf: MIT
- musl libc: MIT
- CA certificate bundle: MPL-2.0 AND MIT
- Docker Engine (Moby) dockerd and docker-proxy (Alpine docker-engine), containerd and
  containerd-shim-runc-v2 (containerd), runc: Apache-2.0. Their LICENSE and NOTICE files
  are in THIRD-PARTY/guest/.
- tini (tini-static, installed as docker-init): MIT
- libseccomp (linked by runc): LGPL-2.1-or-later

Each release archive carries THIRD-PARTY/ (the license texts, generated from the
upstream sources) and SOURCE-OFFER.txt, and each release publishes the corresponding
source of the guest image as hyperbutler-<version>-guest-sources.tar. docs/release.md
describes how these are produced and checked.

Only archives attached to a published GitHub release meet the above. A local
dist/package.ps1 -Snapshot build is not a release: it has no source bundle and no
license texts from the upstream sources, and must not be redistributed.
